\title{Thermodynamics of Mathematical Proof:\\Finite Erasure, Incompressibility, and Adversarial Verification}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 20, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite model connecting proof search, information loss, and Landauer's principle. A candidate derivation of depth $n$ is a binary word of length $n$, so the search space contains exactly $2^n$ candidates. Selecting one candidate and discarding the rest produces erased multiplicity $E(n)=2^n-1$, with recurrence $E(n+1)=2E(n)+1$. This multiplicity dominates the $n$ created choices and exceeds $2n$ for $n\ge4$. If every discarded alternative is represented by an independent bit that is irreversibly reset, the associated work is exactly $kT\ln 2(2^n-1)$. No injective encoding represents every depth-$n$ derivation by a binary string shorter than $n$, and any black-box verifier querying fewer than $2^n$ candidates can miss a uniquely successful derivation. The conclusions are model-relative: the count of alternatives is not automatically Shannon entropy, and the verification bound concerns adversarial unstructured search. Fiber multiplicity emerges as the common invariant behind irreversibility, finite incompressibility, and adversarial ambiguity.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Mathematical proof is abstract, but every process that discovers, verifies, transmits, or stores a proof has a physical realization. Landauer's principle identifies a cost attached not to deduction itself but to logical irreversibility. Resetting one unbiased bit at absolute temperature $T$ requires, in the ideal limit, at least $kT\ln 2$ of dissipated work, where $k$ is Boltzmann's constant. An injective operation can in principle preserve its history. A many-to-one operation merges distinguishable states; if the missing distinction is not retained elsewhere, information is erased.

Proof search suggests such operations. A procedure may explore many candidates and retain one certificate, or normalize many syntactically distinct arguments to a single representative. Nevertheless, candidate count, Shannon entropy, and physical work must be kept separate. A machine may never materialize all rejected branches; candidate probabilities may be nonuniform; and semantic structure may eliminate blind search.

We therefore analyze an explicit finite model. At depth $n$, a candidate derivation is a binary word. Each coordinate records one of two inference choices, giving $2^n$ candidates. Certification retains one and discards the rest. We ask how the discarded population grows, what work follows under a specified independent-bit reset convention, whether all candidates can receive strictly shorter lossless names, and whether a sub-exhaustive black-box verifier can exclude a hidden unique proof.

The answers are exact. The discarded multiplicity is $2^n-1$; its assigned independent-reset work is $kT\ln 2(2^n-1)$; strict uniform compression below $n$ bits is impossible; and fewer than $2^n$ adversarial queries always leave a hiding place. These facts expose a common finite cardinality while preserving the limits of their interpretation.

\section{Finite derivation spaces}

\begin{definition}[Binary derivation space]
For $n\in\mathbb N$, define
\[
D_n=\{0,1\}^n.
\]
An element $(b_1,\ldots,b_n)$ is a candidate derivation of depth $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Candidate count]
For every $n\ge0$, $|D_n|=2^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each of the $n$ coordinates has two independent values, so the multiplication principle gives $2^n$ words. At $n=0$, the unique empty word agrees with $2^0=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Erased multiplicity]
When one distinguished candidate in $D_n$ is retained and every other candidate is discarded, define
\[
E(n)=|D_n|-1.
\]
The term ``erased'' initially denotes logical selection; its physical interpretation will require an explicit representation model.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact erased multiplicity]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
E(n)=2^n-1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $|D_n|=2^n$ into the definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Creation--erasure comparison]
For every $n\ge0$, $n\le E(n)$. For every $n\ge4$,
\[
2n<E(n).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first claim is $n+1\le2^n$ and follows by induction. For the strict claim, the base case is $8<15$. If $2n<2^n-1$, then
\[
2(n+1)=2n+2<2(2^n-1)+1=2^{n+1}-1,
\]
where the middle inequality follows for $n\ge4$.
\end{proof}

The $n$ bits in one retained word record its choices, while $E(n)$ counts the other candidates excluded by selection. This compares description depth with population; it does not yet assign $E(n)$ bits of Shannon information.

\begin{theorem}[One-level recurrence]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
E(n+1)=2E(n)+1.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Compute $2^{n+1}-1=2(2^n-1)+1$. Combinatorially, each old candidate has two extensions: the old rejected population contributes two rejected extensions apiece, and the previously retained candidate contributes one unused sibling.
\end{proof}

\section{Entropy and Landauer accounting}

For a finite probability distribution $p=(p_i)$, write its Shannon entropy in natural units as
\[
H(p)=-\sum_i p_i\ln p_i,
\]
with $0\ln0=0$. Let $u=(1/2,1/2)$ be an unbiased bit and $r=(1,0)$ a reset bit. Direct calculation gives $H(u)=\ln2$ and $H(r)=0$.

\begin{theorem}[One-bit Landauer unit]
At Boltzmann scale $k$ and absolute temperature $T$,
\[
kT\bigl(H(u)-H(r)\bigr)=kT\ln2.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $H(u)=\ln2$ and $H(r)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Independent erasure work]
For $m\in\mathbb N$, define
\[
W(k,T,m)=kT\ln2\,m.
\]
This models the reset of $m$ independently represented unbiased bits.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy-loss representation]
For every $m\ge0$,
\[
W(k,T,m)=kT\bigl(H(u)-H(r)\bigr)m.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the one-bit formula and multiply by $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Proof-selection work]
Assume each discarded candidate contributes one independently represented bit that must be reset. Define
\[
W_n=W(k,T,E(n)).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact proof-selection work]
Under the preceding convention,
\[
W_n=kT\ln2\,(2^n-1).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Insert $E(n)=2^n-1$ into the definition of $W$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear lower comparison]
If $k\ge0$ and $T\ge0$, then
\[
kT\ln2\,n\le W_n
\]
for every $n\ge0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The coefficient $kT\ln2$ is nonnegative, so multiplication preserves $n\le E(n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Representation dependence]
The exponential work formula follows from an exponential number of independently reset records. If a uniformly random candidate is stored only as its $n$-bit index, resetting the index loses $n\ln2$ rather than $(2^n-1)\ln2$. A nonuniform distribution may have still lower entropy. Thus multiplicity, probability, and implementation have distinct roles.
\end{remark}

For a deterministic map $f:X\to Y$, the fiber over $y$ is
\[
f^{-1}(y)=\{x\in X:f(x)=y\}.
\]
A fiber of size $M$ hides $\ln M$ nats when the conditional distribution is uniform. For a nonuniform conditional law, destroyed information is conditional Shannon entropy and is at most $\ln M$. This fiber perspective generalizes the finite selection model without confusing raw counts with physical entropy.

\section{Finite incompressibility}

\begin{definition}[Strictly short descriptions]
Let
\[
S_n=\bigcup_{j=0}^{n-1}\{0,1\}^j
\]
be the binary strings of length strictly less than $n$. For $n=0$, set $S_0=\varnothing$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Short-description count]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
|S_n|=2^n-1.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
There are $2^j$ strings of length $j$, hence
\[
|S_n|=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}2^j=2^n-1.
\]
The empty sum handles $n=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite incompressibility]
For every $n\ge0$, there is no injective map
\[
c:D_n\longrightarrow S_n.
\]
Equivalently, no lossless encoding gives every depth-$n$ derivation a distinct binary description shorter than $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The domain has $2^n$ elements and the codomain has $2^n-1$. The pigeonhole principle excludes an injection from the larger finite set to the smaller.
\end{proof}

This is a uniform claim. Individual structured words may have short descriptions, but not all words can enjoy strict savings simultaneously. The result is a finite precursor to Kolmogorov incompressibility and avoids dependence on a selected universal machine. It also implies that every lossless variable-length code for $D_n$ assigns length at least $n$ to some candidate.

\section{Adversarial verification}

Let a verifier query a subset $Q\subseteq D_n$. A query reveals whether that candidate succeeds. No structure is assumed among answers; an adversary may place a unique success anywhere consistent with the transcript.

\begin{theorem}[Adversarial coverage]
If $Q\subseteq D_n$ and $|Q|<2^n$, then there is $p\in D_n\setminus Q$ such that declaring $p$ uniquely successful and every queried candidate unsuccessful is consistent with an all-negative transcript on $Q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Because $|Q|<|D_n|$, the set $Q$ is proper. Choose $p\notin Q$. Assign success only to $p$. Every answer on $Q$ is negative, while the successful candidate remains unqueried.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Worst-case exhaustive requirement]
In the unstructured black-box model, ruling out a unique successful depth-$n$ candidate after negative answers requires $2^n$ queries in the worst case.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Every smaller query set is defeated by the theorem, while querying all candidates suffices.
\end{proof}

This result concerns black-box search, not checking a supplied certificate. A semantic checker can exploit local inference rules and may run in time polynomial in proof length. The adversarial theorem applies precisely when validity is an arbitrary hidden predicate.

\section{Unified witness theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential erasure witness]
For every integer $n\ge4$, the binary derivation family $D_n$ has all of the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item selecting one candidate discards exactly $E(n)=2^n-1$ alternatives;
\item the discarded multiplicity satisfies $2n<E(n)$;
\item no injective encoding maps all candidates to binary strings of length below $n$;
\item every query set $Q\subseteq D_n$ with $|Q|<2^n$ omits a candidate that can be made uniquely successful while all queried candidates are unsuccessful.
\end{enumerate}
Under the independent-bit reset convention, selection work is $kT\ln2(2^n-1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The four items are respectively the exact multiplicity theorem, the strict creation--erasure comparison, finite incompressibility, and adversarial coverage. The work identity follows from the exact proof-selection work theorem.
\end{proof}

The boundary $n\ge4$ is a convenient sufficient condition. In fact $2n<2^n-1$ already holds at $n=3$, but not at every smaller depth. All exact counting and incompressibility statements remain valid for every $n\ge0$.

\section{Algorithms and numerical exploration}

Direct accounting computes $2^n-1$ and then multiplies by $kT\ln2$. Exponentiation by squaring uses $O(\log n)$ arbitrary-precision integer multiplications, with an unavoidable output length of $O(n)$ bits. The first values are shown below.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rrrr}
\toprule
$n$ & candidates & $E(n)$ & $E(n)>2n$ \\
\midrule
0 & 1 & 0 & no \\
1 & 2 & 1 & no \\
2 & 4 & 3 & no \\
3 & 8 & 7 & yes \\
4 & 16 & 15 & yes \\
5 & 32 & 31 & yes \\
10 & 1024 & 1023 & yes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

A computational demonstration of incompressibility assigns $2^n$ derivations to only $2^n-1$ short descriptions and records the first collision. With a hash table this takes $O(2^n)$ assignments and memory in the worst case, apart from word-length factors. The collision is guaranteed for every total assignment, not merely for a particular algorithm.

For adversarial coverage, store queried words in a hash set and enumerate $D_n$ until finding the first omitted word. At most $2^n$ membership tests are needed. If words are represented by integers from $0$ to $2^n-1$, one may instead sort the queried integers in $O(|Q|\log|Q|)$ time and locate a gap.

\section{Applications}

\subsection{Proof normalization}

A normalization map sends proof terms to canonical representatives. Its fibers collect syntactically distinct terms that normalize to the same result. A reversible implementation must preserve enough side information to identify the original term within its fiber. If that information is reset, conditional entropy governs ideal work. A concrete extension of the present model would construct a terminating calculus with short conclusions and exponentially large normalization fibers.

\subsection{Reversible verification}

A verifier can avoid erasure by retaining its transcript. This uses memory. With limited memory it may keep checkpoints and recompute deleted segments, creating a time--space tradeoff analogous to reversible pebble games. The adversarial coverage theorem measures the static information that unstructured verification must distinguish; a reversible analysis would quantify the cost of preserving or reconstructing it.

\subsection{Kolmogorov complexity}

Finite incompressibility compares sets directly. Prefix-free Kolmogorov complexity fixes a universal machine and measures shortest program length. Counting then implies that many $n$-bit strings have complexity near $n$, up to machine constants. A thermodynamic extension would identify whether information missing from a short certificate enters through the verifier's input transcript, retained state, or irreversible changes.

\subsection{Shared lemmas}

Independent obligations have additive entropy under product distributions, so ideal erasure work adds. Shared lemmas correlate obligations and permit information reuse. Mutual information, rather than syntax count, should measure the reduction in total work. This suggests a compositional theory of the thermodynamic value of reusable arguments.

\section{Limitations and future work}

Candidate multiplicity is not proof difficulty: semantics may guide search. Discarded candidates are not automatically independent bits: the exponential work formula belongs to a specific representation. Adversarial coverage concerns black-box validity, not ordinary certificate checking. Finite incompressibility excludes universal strict compression, not compression of a chosen structured proof.

Five extensions are especially natural. First, for finite terminating normalization systems, expected destroyed information should equal conditional entropy; expected log fiber size should be attained exactly for uniform conditional distributions. Second, reversible verification should exhibit a Bennett-style tradeoff among transcript erasure, checkpoint space, and recomputation time. Third, an explicit strongly normalizing calculus should realize exponential fibers over short normal proofs. Fourth, finite incompressibility should be lifted to universal prefix-free complexity with explicit additive constants. Fifth, compositional accounting should show additivity for independent obligations and quantify savings from shared lemmas by mutual information.

\section{Conclusion}

At depth $n$, the finite binary model contains $2^n$ candidate derivations. Selecting one leaves $2^n-1$ alternatives; no strictly shorter binary code names every candidate uniquely; and any sub-exhaustive black-box transcript leaves room for a hidden unique success. Under an explicit independent-record reset model, the same multiplicity yields work $kT\ln2(2^n-1)$.

The unifying object is the family of distinctions collapsed by a physical operation. Logical maps determine which inputs become indistinguishable, probability laws determine how much information is lost, and implementations determine whether that information is retained, recomputed, or erased. Keeping those roles separate turns the thermodynamics of mathematical reasoning into a precise finite theory.

\end{document}

